          <div class="area-item fade-in" id="knowledge">
            <div class="area-item-header">
              <span class="area-item-num">01</span>
              <h3>Institutional Knowledge Documentation</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Twenty years of relationship instinct. Pricing logic that took a decade to calibrate.
              Vendor dynamics, exception handling, the judgment calls that only come from having
              been in the room — it's all concentrated in the people who built this business.
              That's a strength. It's also an asset that deserves to be protected.
            </p>
            <p>
              We capture all of it. Customer relationship maps, pricing frameworks, operational
              decision trees, supplier agreements and the informal terms that actually govern them.
              We put it somewhere it can be passed on — to the next leader, the next owner,
              or the team that's already here and ready to carry it forward.
            </p>
            <p>
              This is the foundational work. Everything else — automation, succession, valuation —
              becomes more valuable once the knowledge is documented. It's also the single
              highest-leverage thing an owner can do to protect what they've spent a lifetime building.
            </p>
            <div class="highlight">
              <strong>What we deliver:</strong> A documented knowledge archive structured for
              operational continuity — not a filing cabinet, but a working resource the next
              person in the role can actually use on day one.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="area-item fade-in" id="automation">
            <div class="area-item-header">
              <span class="area-item-num">02</span>
              <h3>Administrative Task Automation</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              A $15M company typically has an office manager or operations coordinator spending
              15–20 hours a week on work that could be 3–4. Manual invoicing. Chasing approvals
              through email chains. Pulling weekly reports from three different systems by hand.
              Scheduling and dispatch phone tag. Purchase order routing. Collections follow-up.
            </p>
            <p>
              The automation isn't exotic. In most cases it means connecting the tools the
              business already owns and stopping the copy-paste. We identify which manual
              processes are candidates, map the current flow, and design the replacement —
              then document it so it doesn't depend on one person knowing how it works.
            </p>
            <p>
              The framing matters: this isn't about eliminating roles. It's about returning
              capacity to the people who already work here. The office manager who spends
              4 hours a week on reports instead of 14 can do something more valuable with
              the other 10. That's the conversation we have with your team.
            </p>
            <div class="highlight">
              <strong>Typical result:</strong> 10–15 hours per week of manual administrative work
              returned to the business within the first 90 days — without adding headcount
              or replacing systems.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="area-item fade-in" id="revenue">
            <div class="area-item-header">
              <span class="area-item-num">03</span>
              <h3>Revenue You've Already Earned</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              In businesses this size, there's typically a meaningful gap between what's being
              delivered and what's being fully captured. Unbilled time and materials, inconsistent
              pricing across the team, invoices going out late, discounts without policy,
              contracts that renewed quietly without a conversation. None of it requires new
              sales to fix. It's already in the business — it just needs to be collected.
            </p>
            <p>
              We map where revenue is slipping through, document the pattern, and show you
              the math. Then we work with your team to close the gap systematically —
              without adding friction to the customer relationship.
            </p>
            <div class="highlight">
              <strong>Common finding:</strong> Pricing inconsistency — the owner charges one rate,
              the team charges another, and nobody has noticed because the business is busy.
              Standardizing it often recovers 2–4% of revenue with no new sales required.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="area-item fade-in" id="process">
            <div class="area-item-header">
              <span class="area-item-num">04</span>
              <h3>Process Standardization</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              The best businesses in this size range run on the expertise of the people who
              built them. That's the strength. Process standardization takes what your best
              people already do and makes it replicable — so the quality holds regardless of
              who's in the room, and the knowledge doesn't walk out the door when someone leaves.
            </p>
            <p>
              We document how things actually get done — the real process, not the org chart
              version — then work with your team to standardize what works and simplify what
              doesn't. The result is a business where a new hire can get up to speed in days
              instead of months, and where your standards travel without you having to carry them.
            </p>
            <div class="highlight">
              <strong>Why this matters for valuation:</strong> A buyer due diligence team
              will ask to see documented processes. A business that can show its own playbook
              commands a premium. One that says "it lives with our people" leaves money on the table.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="area-item fade-in" id="waste">
            <div class="area-item-header">
              <span class="area-item-num">05</span>
              <h3>Hidden Operational Profit</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              Five percent is a conservative estimate of the profit available inside businesses
              this size — already in the business, waiting to be unlocked. On a $20M company,
              that's $1 million: in vendor contracts that were competitive once, software
              subscriptions that never got reviewed, purchasing without negotiation leverage,
              and process steps that made sense when the business was half this size.
            </p>
            <p>
              We find it, document it, and show you the verified math. We don't recommend
              changes without showing the current cost and the alternative. Every finding
              comes with a number attached.
            </p>
            <div class="highlight">
              <strong>What we deliver:</strong> A prioritized opportunity register — every identified
              source of hidden profit with its estimated annual value, confidence level, and what it
              would take to capture it. No guesses. No round numbers.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="area-item fade-in" id="valuation">
            <div class="area-item-header">
              <span class="area-item-num">06</span>
              <h3>Valuation and Succession Positioning</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              An owner-driven business sells at 2–3× EBITDA.
              A business with documented processes, systematized customer relationships,
              and a leadership team that can carry the work forward sells at 4–5×.
              On a $200K–$300K EBITDA business, that difference is $400K–$600K of real family wealth.
            </p>
            <p>
              We show you what your business is worth today — with the numbers to back it up —
              and what it could be worth in 18 months with the right work in place. The entry
              conversation isn't "you should sell." It's "let me show you what this is actually
              worth, and what it could be."
            </p>
            <p>
              For owners approaching a transition, we structure the engagement to produce
              documentation that holds up in front of a buyer's due diligence team, a
              banker, or a business broker. The assessment deliverable is designed to
              be that document.
            </p>
            <div class="highlight">
              <strong>The 18-month case:</strong> Document the processes. Reduce owner dependency.
              Systematize customer relationships. Present a clean operations picture.
              The sale decision takes care of itself once the numbers are on the table.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="area-item fade-in" id="infrastructure">
            <div class="area-item-header">
              <span class="area-item-num">07</span>
              <h3>Infrastructure &amp; Operational Efficiency</h3>
            </div>
            <p>
              In most businesses this size, the physical assets — equipment, vehicles, facilities —
              are managed reactively. Something breaks, you fix it. Something wears out, you replace it
              when the crisis forces the decision. The cost of that approach is rarely calculated:
              unplanned downtime, emergency repair premiums, and the revenue that doesn't get delivered
              while the press is down, the truck is in the shop, or the line is idle.
            </p>
            <p>
              There's a second cost that's even easier to miss: production waste. Misalignment in a
              print run. Material spoilage in food production. Rework in fabrication. Every business
              that touches physical goods has a waste rate. Few have measured it with enough precision
              to manage it. The businesses that have — even reducing it by two or three points — find
              that the savings go straight to the bottom line.
            </p>
            <p>
              We also look at the capital investment picture in the context of where the owner is in
              their journey. A major equipment purchase is a 15-year decision. That decision looks
              very different if you're planning to transition the business in five. We help owners
              see the full picture before committing — and help them build the asset profile that
              a buyer sees as a strength rather than a question mark.
            </p>
            <div class="highlight">
              <strong>What we find:</strong> Equipment reliability risk, quantified downtime costs,
              material waste rates with dollar values attached, and a capital investment timeline
              aligned with the owner's actual horizon — not just the next invoice cycle.
            </div>
          </div>

